consiSt("vt",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Vermont Supreme Court","d":"Vermont’s only appellate court; there is no intermediate court of appeals. Five justices sit in Montpelier (109 State Street). It hears appeals from the Superior Court and from state agencies and boards, decides some matters in its original jurisdiction, adopts the rules of procedure, and controls admission and discipline of lawyers and judges. Some appeals go to three-justice panels, which usually rule within days; full-court cases take months. Its decisions on state law are final; only federal questions can go on to the U.S. Supreme Court."},{"l":"TRIAL COURT","n":"Vermont Superior Court (14 county units)","d":"One unified trial court with a unit in each of Vermont’s 14 counties. Divisions: Civil, Criminal, Family, Probate, and the statewide Environmental Division. Civil cases go to the Civil Division of the county unit."},{"l":"CIVIL DIVISION","n":"Superior Court, Civil Division (including Small Claims)","d":"General civil trial court: contract, tort, eviction, foreclosure, land disputes, malpractice, and appeals from the Probate Division. Small claims go up to $10,000, but consumer-credit and medical-debt claims are capped at $5,000. Post-conviction relief petitions are also filed here (V.R.C.P. 80.12, effective January 1, 2027)."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED","n":"Environmental Division; Family Division; Probate Division","d":"The Environmental Division is statewide and hears environmental and land-use cases, including appeals from municipal panels. The Family Division handles divorce, parentage, support, juvenile, and abuse-prevention cases, with no jury trials. The Probate Division handles estates, trusts, guardianships, and adoptions; its 14 judges are elected to four-year terms."},{"l":"LIMITED","n":"Judicial Bureau","d":"Statewide tribunal for civil violations such as traffic tickets, municipal ordinance violations, and fish-and-game violations."}],"slapp":[{"t":"12 V.S.A. § 1041: special motion to strike","d":"Vermont’s anti-SLAPP statute lets a defendant file a special motion to strike a claim arising from the defendant’s exercise, in connection with a public issue, of the right of free speech or the right to petition the government under the U.S. or Vermont Constitution.\nProtected activity includes: statements made before a legislative, executive, or judicial proceeding or other official proceeding; statements made in connection with an issue under review by such a body; statements on an issue of public interest made in a public forum or a place open to the public; and any other statement or conduct on a public issue or issue of public interest that furthers free speech or petition rights.\nThe statute was last amended by 2023, No. 14, § 2 (effective May 10, 2023). The legislature’s site says it includes the 2026 session, and it shows no 2024–2026 amendment to § 1041.","c":"12 V.S.A. § 1041(a), (i); history note (2005, No. 134; 2023, No. 14)","v":true},{"t":"Deadline: 60 days from filing of the complaint; hearing within 30 days","d":"File and serve the motion within 60 days after the complaint is filed. The clock runs from filing, not from service. The plaintiff may respond within 15 days after service of the motion. The court may extend these times for good cause.\nThe court must hold a hearing within 30 days after the motion is served, unless good cause supports an extension.","c":"12 V.S.A. § 1041(b), (d)","v":true},{"t":"Discovery stay","d":"Filing the motion stays all discovery in the action until the court rules. On motion and for good cause, the court may allow limited discovery to help it decide the motion.","c":"12 V.S.A. § 1041(c)","v":true},{"t":"Standard: plaintiff must show no reasonable basis AND actual injury","d":"The court must grant the motion unless the plaintiff shows both that (A) the defendant’s exercise of speech or petition rights was devoid of any reasonable factual support and any arguable basis in law, and (B) the defendant’s acts caused actual injury to the plaintiff. The court decides on the pleadings and on supporting and opposing affidavits.\nThe ruling, and the fact that the court made it, are not admissible later in the case and do not change any burden or degree of proof.","c":"12 V.S.A. § 1041(e), (f)(2)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: mandatory for a winning defendant; two-way for frivolous motions","d":"If the motion is granted, the court must award costs and reasonable attorney’s fees to the defendant. If the motion is denied and the court finds it frivolous or meant only to cause delay, the court must award costs and fees to the plaintiff.\nSeparately, 12 V.S.A. § 5771 lets the court award costs and fees to a defendant who wins a defamation action that it finds frivolous and without merit.","c":"12 V.S.A. § 1041(f)(1); 12 V.S.A. § 5771","v":true},{"t":"Appeal; exclusions","d":"An order granting or denying the motion is appealable in the same manner as an interlocutory order under V.R.A.P. 5. The statute does not say more. Check whether the appellate courts treat this as an appeal as of right or one that still requires the V.R.A.P. 5 permission procedure; case law was not collected.\nThe statute does not apply to (1) enforcement actions or criminal proceedings brought by the State or a political subdivision, or (2) cases of tortious interference with legally protected health care under 12 V.S.A. § 7302.","c":"12 V.S.A. § 1041(g), (h)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice is a two-step process: license first, then motion (not Admission Rule 18)","d":"Pro hac vice is governed by Administrative Order No. 41, § 16 and V.R.C.P. 79.1(e), not by the Rules of Admission. Admission Rule 18 deals with a character-and-fitness panel’s decision after a hearing.\nStep 1: A lawyer admitted in another state or D.C., and not suspended or disbarred anywhere, must file a pro hac vice licensing statement under oath with the State Court Administrator before moving for admission. Attach a certificate of good standing from one admitting state and pay the fee. File a separate statement and pay a separate fee for each case.\nThe fee is set by the State Court Administrator’s fee schedule, which was not collected, so the amount is not stated here. The Administrator may waive it for pro bono representation of indigent clients.\nStep 2: File the pro hac vice licensing card in the court where the case is pending, with a motion by a Vermont bar member. Parallel rules: V.R.Cr.P. 44.2(b), V.R.F.P. 15(e), V.R.P.P. 79.1(d), V.R.A.P. 45.1(d).","c":"A.O. 41, § 16(a)–(c) (version updated through January 2026); V.R.C.P. 79.1(e)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel must stay in the case and sign everything","d":"The motion is made by a Vermont bar member who is actively associated with the out-of-state lawyer, and it must say who will be lead counsel. Admission is in the court’s discretion and may be revoked at any time for good cause.\nThroughout the case, the Vermont lawyer must sign every document filed, receives service of all process, notices, and documents, and may be required to attend.\nThe admitted lawyer is bound by Vermont statutes and Supreme Court rules, including the Rules of Professional Conduct and A.O. 9 (Professional Responsibility Program).","c":"V.R.C.P. 79.1(e) (text as amended effective Sept. 6, 2022); A.O. 41, § 16(d)","v":true},{"t":"Limited appearances are expressly allowed","d":"Under a limited-representation agreement that complies with the Rules of Professional Conduct, a lawyer may appear for a self-represented client for listed purposes only. The purposes are: filing a pleading; a specific motion; specific discovery; a pretrial conference or ADR session; a particular hearing or trial; taking and perfecting an appeal; or, with leave, a specific issue or part of a trial.\nFile and serve a notice of limited appearance describing the scope, stating that the client is self-represented, before the work begins. The notice is subject to Rule 11.\nWithdrawal is a matter of course once the purpose is done. Earlier withdrawal needs a motion and good cause. Until the lawyer withdraws, papers go to both the client and the lawyer.","c":"V.R.C.P. 79.1(h)(1)–(4)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing is mandatory for lawyers (eFile & Serve)","d":"All documents in the Supreme Court, Superior Court, Judicial Bureau, and Professional Responsibility Board hearing panels must be e-filed, subject to listed exceptions. The Judiciary says attorneys must use eFile & Serve (EFS) for all filings in all divisions. EFS is the Tyler platform that replaced Odyssey File & Serve. The page says OFS stays available until end of day September 25 but gives no year.\nUser fee: $14 per filer or firm per case, charged at the first filing and again when representation changes. Card payments carry a 2.89% processing fee; eCheck is $1.\nTo be served, add your service contact to the public list in EFS. Court notices and orders go to the email addresses registered in eCabinet (up to three). Register through the Judiciary help desk with your bar number.","c":"2020 V.R.E.F. 3(a)–(b), 5(b)(7); Vermont Judiciary E-Filing page (accessed Nov. 10, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Admission without exam: 5 of the last 10 years (3 for Maine/New Hampshire lawyers)","d":"An attorney-applicant must have been actively engaged in practice for 5 of the preceding 10 years in U.S. jurisdictions. Part of that may be waived down to 3 of the last 10 years if a jurisdiction where the applicant is licensed, and has practiced at least 6 months, requires fewer than 5 years for its own admission on motion.\nLawyers licensed in Maine or New Hampshire who have practiced there for the 3 years right before applying can use a 3-year rule, for as long as those states keep an equivalent rule.\nApplicants are barred if they failed the Vermont exam, or failed to earn a passing UBE score, within the past 5 years. Post-admission CLE under Rule 12 applies.","c":"Vt. R. Admission 14, 15(a)–(c) (version effective July 1, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"UBE transfer: 270 (legacy) or 620 (NextGen); MPRE 80","d":"A transferred UBE score must be 270 or higher on the legacy UBE, or 620 or higher on the NextGen UBE. It must be earned within 5 years before the application, or at the next administration after filing. The MPRE score must be 80 or higher, earned from 5 years before to 1 year after filing.\nThe rule that a score must come within 4 sittings is deleted effective January 1, 2027 (order of October 27, 2026).","c":"Vt. R. Admission 9(b)(2), 13(b)–(d); Promulgation Order amending Rules 9(b)(3) and 13 (Oct. 27, 2026, eff. Jan. 1, 2027)","v":true},{"t":"New: provisional licensure while character and fitness review is pending","d":"Since July 1, 2026, an applicant for admission who is licensed and in good standing in another U.S. jurisdiction, and who has been found eligible by the Board of Bar Examiners, may practice law in Vermont on a provisional basis while waiting for the Character and Fitness report or admission.\nThe applicant must first file a form with the Office of Attorney Licensing agreeing to be bound by Vermont rules, including the Rules of Professional Conduct. Provisional licensure ends on full licensure, withdrawal, denial, or loss of eligibility.","c":"A.O. 41, § 19 (added by order of Jan. 6, 2026, eff. July 1, 2026)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Vermont Rules of Professional Conduct (text not collected)","d":"Vermont lawyers are governed by the Vermont Rules of Professional Conduct (V.R.Pr.C.), which other collected rules cite by Model Rule-style numbers (Rules 1.16, 5.5, and 6.1 cmt. [4]).\nThe Judiciary publishes the full current text only through LexisNexis, which this project’s sourcing rules exclude, so the rules were not collected. No differences from the ABA Model Rules are stated here. Check the Lexis-hosted official text before relying on any specific rule.","c":"A.O. 41, §§ 1, 16(d); V.R.Pr.C. (not collected)","v":false},{"t":"Unauthorized practice is contempt; Rule 5.5 sets the exceptions","d":"Practicing law in Vermont without a license is prohibited and may be punished as contempt, unless Rule 5.5 of the Vermont Rules of Professional Conduct authorizes it. The text of Vermont’s Rule 5.5 was not collected, so compare it with your home state’s temporary-practice rule before doing any Vermont work.","c":"A.O. 41, § 1","v":true},{"t":"Discipline: Professional Responsibility Program (A.O. 9), recently restructured","d":"Lawyer discipline runs through the Professional Responsibility Program under Administrative Order No. 9.\nSince January 1, 2026, Bar Counsel screens all disciplinary complaints, replacing Screening Counsel. A complaint that does not justify investigation or prosecution, but should not simply be dismissed, may be referred to the Bar Assistance Program for nondisciplinary resolution.\nSince July 1, 2025, a complainant must ask for review of a decision to close or dismiss within 30 days, and review is on the record.\nFurther amendments to A.O. 9 Rules 13.D, 15, 16.B, 17.C, 18, and 27.A took effect July 1, 2026; read the order for details.","c":"A.O. 9, Rules 2, 5, 6.C–D, 12 (order of Oct. 2025, eff. Jan. 1, 2026); A.O. 9, Rule 12.D (eff. July 1, 2025); A.O. 9 order of Feb. 10, 2026 (eff. July 1, 2026)","v":true}],"style":"State of Vermont","home":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Anti-SLAPP clock runs from the complaint’s filing, not service","d":"The special motion to strike is due 60 days after the complaint is filed. If service was late, much of that time may be gone before you are retained. Calendar it from the docket date.","c":"12 V.S.A. § 1041(b)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice needs a license from the Court Administrator before the motion","d":"You cannot just have local counsel move for admission. First file the sworn pro hac vice licensing statement, with a good-standing certificate and fee, with the State Court Administrator, separately for each case. Then file the licensing card with local counsel’s motion. Local counsel must sign every filing for the life of the case.","c":"A.O. 41, § 16; V.R.C.P. 79.1(e)"},{"t":"No three extra days for mailed service","d":"Vermont abolished its ‘three-day rule’ across all courts effective September 6, 2022. Response periods triggered by service are not extended because service was by mail. To compensate, appellate briefing periods were lengthened (see appeals).","c":"Vermont Judiciary news release on Sept. 6, 2022 amendments (former V.R.C.P. 6(e), V.R.A.P. 26(c), V.R.Cr.P. 45(e))"},{"t":"Notice of appeal goes to the trial court, and the clock is jurisdictional","d":"File the notice of appeal with the Superior Court clerk (or the agency) within 30 days of entry, not with the Supreme Court. The Supreme Court treats timely filing as jurisdictional.\nThen, within 14 days of filing the notice, file the docketing statement in the Supreme Court and order transcripts.","c":"V.R.A.P. 3(e), 4(a)(1), 10(b)(1); 12 V.S.A. § 2383; In re Petition of VT Real Estate Holdings 1 LLC, 2026 VT 13, ¶ 4"},{"t":"Printed case is back: file it with the opening brief","d":"Since January 1, 2024, the appellant must file a printed case for most appeals, even when there is an appeal volume. It is due at the same time as the appellant’s principal brief, and briefs must cite to it. No printed case is required in appeals from small claims, the Human Services Board, or the Employment Security Board, and the Court may waive it.","c":"V.R.A.P. 28(c), 30(a)(1)(B), 30(e) (eff. July 1, 2025)"},{"t":"Preliminary injunction needs a separate motion","d":"Since January 1, 2026, you can no longer apply for a preliminary injunction just by including it in the complaint. A motion is required, and the movant must serve the notice of hearing with the motion on all parties.","c":"V.R.C.P. 65(b)(1) (eff. Jan. 1, 2026)"},{"t":"Default judgment on out-of-state service: cite the foreign law","d":"From January 1, 2027, a motion for default judgment that relies on service outside Vermont under the law of the place of service must name the statute or rule of that place that authorizes the method used.","c":"V.R.C.P. 55(a) (eff. Jan. 1, 2027)"},{"t":"Summary judgment: response must reproduce each numbered fact","d":"The movant files a separate statement of undisputed material facts in numbered paragraphs with record cites. The opponent must respond paragraph by paragraph, reproducing each numbered paragraph before its response, and may add a separate statement of additional facts. On request, each side must provide an editable copy.","c":"V.R.C.P. 56(c)(1)–(2)"},{"t":"E-filing rejections: 7 days to fix and keep your filing date","d":"If court staff reject an e-filing or the submission fails, you may file a corrected version within 7 days of the notice and keep the original submission date. It is your burden to prove the rejection date. You may appeal a rejection to the Court Administrator within 7 days, which tolls the correction period.\nYour own computer or internet failure does not excuse a missed deadline.","c":"2020 V.R.E.F. 5(c)(2), 5(d)(4), (5), (7), 5(e)(4), (7)"},{"t":"One motion per document","d":"Every motion must be filed as a separate lead document. You may not respond to a motion and make a new motion in the same document. Affidavits and exhibits must be filed separately from the motion; a compiled exhibit file needs page numbers that match the PDF counter and a table of contents.","c":"2020 V.R.E.F. 5(g)(1)–(2)"},{"t":"No live external links in filed documents or briefs","d":"E-filed documents and briefs may not contain live external hyperlinks. Internal links within the document are allowed from January 1, 2027. Briefs must use 13-point serif font, 1.2 line spacing, left alignment, one-inch margins, and page numbers centered at the bottom.","c":"2020 V.R.E.F. 7(a)(7); V.R.A.P. 32(a)(1)(D)–(E) (amended eff. Jan. 1, 2027)"},{"t":"Reconsideration may or may not toll the appeal clock, depending on the forum","d":"A timely motion to alter or amend filed with the Public Utility Commission tolls the time to appeal to the Supreme Court under V.R.A.P. 4(b)(5). An informal request to a town zoning board to reconsider did not toll the time to appeal to the Environmental Division, because the Environmental Court rules do not incorporate Appellate Rule 4(b) tolling. Check the forum’s rules before relying on tolling.","c":"In re Petition of VT Real Estate Holdings 1 LLC, 2026 VT 13, ¶¶ 5–10 (discussing In re Jersey Street CU Reconsideration Denial, 2024 VT 20); V.R.E.C.P. 5(b)(1)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory substitution found; disqualification for interest or kinship","d":"Statute bars a justice, judge, or juror from acting in a case where they were counsel, have an interest in the outcome, or are related to a party within the fourth degree of consanguinity or affinity. For a corporate party, the bar covers relatives of its officers, directors, trustees, or agents. Being a taxpayer or resident of a municipality that is a party is not disqualifying.\nA judge may not sit en banc on a case the judge tried below.\nThe collected texts contain no peremptory change-of-judge procedure. Because the civil rules and the Code of Judicial Conduct were not collected in full, the absence of one is not confirmed. Check V.R.C.P. 40 and the Code of Judicial Conduct before relying on that.","c":"12 V.S.A. §§ 61, 63","v":false},{"t":"Post-conviction relief: never the sentencing judge","d":"Under new Civil Rule 80.12, the judge who reviews a post-conviction relief petition may not be the judge who imposed the sentence or entered the challenged judgment.","c":"V.R.C.P. 80.12(e)(1) (eff. Jan. 1, 2027)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Vermont Constitution speech protections: not established here","d":"The anti-SLAPP statute protects petition and speech rights under both the U.S. and the Vermont Constitutions. The Vermont Constitution’s text and case law on whether it protects speech more broadly than the First Amendment were not collected.","c":"12 V.S.A. § 1041(a) (refers to the Vermont Constitution); Vt. Const. not collected","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Court rules are subject to a legislative check","d":"The Supreme Court makes procedural rules under 12 V.S.A. § 1. They take effect on the date in the promulgation order unless the Legislative Committee on Judicial Rules objects. The General Assembly may repeal or revise any rule, and later rules cannot override its action. Promulgation orders usually take effect January 1 or July 1.","c":"12 V.S.A. §§ 1, 3, 4; see Order making V.R.C.P. 9.2 permanent (June 2, 2025) (referring to A.O. 11 preferred effective dates)","v":true},{"t":"Declarations replace affidavits in civil cases","d":"Since July 1, 2025, whenever a civil rule or court order calls for an affidavit or sworn statement, a party may file a declaration signed under penalty of perjury using the language in Rule 43(h). Summary-judgment declarations must be on personal knowledge. Rule 43(h) does not cover acknowledgments and other notarizations.","c":"V.R.C.P. 43(h), 56(c)(6) (eff. July 1, 2025)","v":true},{"t":"Certificate of merit in medical-malpractice cases","d":"A medical-negligence complaint must be filed together with a certificate of merit stating that a qualified health care provider was consulted and found a reasonable likelihood of breach and causation, for each defendant. The clerk grants an automatic 90-day extension of the limitations period on petition so the consultation can be done. Without a certificate, the case is dismissed without prejudice, except in the rare cases where no expert testimony is needed. The certificate is not required for informed-consent-only claims.","c":"12 V.S.A. § 1042","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Sources: Vermont Rules of Appellate Procedure (V.R.A.P.), compiled from official Vermont Supreme Court orders on vermontjudiciary.org: the July 2021 Emergency Order amending Rules 1, 3–6, 9–14, 21, 24–28, 30–32, 34, 39, 44, 45, and 45.1 (full rule text); the orders amending Rule 30(b) and (e) (eff. July 1, 2025), Rules 10(b)(8)(A) and 31(a)(1) (eff. Jan. 1, 2026), and Rule 32(a)(1)(E) (eff. Jan. 1, 2027); the Supreme Court’s ‘Appealing to the Supreme Court’ page and its timeline (accessed Nov. 10, 2026); Judiciary news releases on the September 6, 2022 time-period changes and the January 1, 2024 printed-case rule; In re Petition of VT Real Estate Holdings 1 LLC, 2026 VT 13; 12 V.S.A. §§ 1041(g), 2383, 2386; and the 2020 Vermont Rules for Electronic Filing (amended through July 1, 2026).\nThe Judiciary publishes the full current V.R.A.P. only through LexisNexis, which was not used. Where a rule was amended after 2021 and the amending order was not collected (notably Rules 26 and 40, and the 2022 order that extended briefing times), this page relies on the Judiciary’s own summaries and says so.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Vermont: one appellate court, a 30-day jurisdictional clock, and filing in the trial court","d":"Vermont has no intermediate appellate court. Appeals from the Superior Court’s divisions and from state agencies go straight to the Vermont Supreme Court, which decides some cases in three-justice panels and others as a full court. Its state-law rulings are final.\nThe notice of appeal is filed with the trial court or agency within 30 days after entry of the judgment. Timely filing is jurisdictional. Within 14 days of the notice, the appellant files a docketing statement in the Supreme Court and orders transcripts. Briefing starts when the Court gives notice that the record is complete.\nSince September 6, 2022, no extra three days are added for mailed service, and brief deadlines were extended to 40/30/21 days.","c":"V.R.A.P. 3(e), 4(a), 10(b)(1), 31(a); 12 V.S.A. § 2383; 2026 VT 13, ¶ 4"},"groups":[["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Notice of appeal: 30 days, filed in the trial court","d":"The notice required by Rule 3 must be filed with the superior court clerk within 30 days after entry of the judgment or order appealed from. Statute sets the same 30-day period for appeals from courts, commissions, boards, and agencies unless another law provides otherwise.\nIf the notice is mistakenly filed in the Supreme Court, the Supreme Court clerk notes the receipt date and forwards it; it counts as filed in the trial court on that date. A notice filed after a decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the date of entry.\nE-filers should copy supremecourt@vtcourts.gov using the courtesy-copy feature. Pay the filing fee to the trial court, or apply for a waiver there.","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(a)(1), (3), (5); 12 V.S.A. § 2383; Vermont Supreme Court, Appealing to the Supreme Court (web page)"},{"t":"Shorter periods for the State in criminal cases and some civil matters","d":"In a criminal case, the State must appeal within 7 business days after entry. If the sentence is life imprisonment and the defendant has not waived appeal, the State has 30 days.\nThe Court’s guidance says some case types, including foreclosure judgments, small claims, and traffic court (Judicial Bureau) appeals, require a request to appeal within 14 days. The underlying rules for those case types were not collected, so check them.","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(a)(2); Vermont Supreme Court, Appealing to the Supreme Court (web page)"},{"t":"Docketing statement and transcripts within 14 days","d":"The appellant files a docketing statement in the Supreme Court within 14 days after the notice of appeal is filed. The appellee may file one within 14 days after the appellant’s.\nAlso within 14 days, the appellant orders the transcripts needed to review the issues, or says on the docketing statement that none are needed. In bail appeals and juvenile abuse-and-neglect cases, transcripts must be ordered when the notice is filed.\nIf the appellee wants more hearings transcribed, the appellee files and serves a list. The appellant then has 14 days to order them, failing which the appellee may order them at its own expense.\nA proposed amendment to Rule 3(e) would make the appellee’s docketing statement optional; whether it was adopted was not confirmed.","c":"V.R.A.P. 3(e), 10(b)(1), (4), (5); Supreme Court timeline; Proposed Order Amending V.R.A.P. 3(e) and 27(a)(3) (status unverified)"},{"t":"Record: appeal volume, then ‘record complete’ notice","d":"The trial court sends the appeal to the Supreme Court within 14 days after the notice is filed. The Supreme Court opens the case and, where applicable, prepares an appeal volume (a PDF of the electronic trial-court record) within 14 days.\nWhen transcripts are done, or none are needed, the Court sends notice that the record is complete. That notice starts the briefing clock.\nIn qualifying fee-waiver cases with 4 hours or less of digitally recorded proceedings, the appellant may ask that the audio recording be accepted in place of a transcript.","c":"V.R.A.P. 10(b)(8)(A) (eff. Jan. 1, 2026), 11(b)(1), 12(a)–(b); Supreme Court timeline"}]],["Tolling, extensions, and cross-appeals",[{"t":"Post-judgment motions that restart the clock","d":"If a party timely files one of the listed motions, the full appeal period runs for all parties from entry of the order disposing of the last remaining motion. Listed civil motions: V.R.C.P. 50(b) judgment as a matter of law; denial of a Rule 50(c)(2) new-trial motion; a Rule 52(b) motion to amend findings; a Rule 54 fee motion if the court so orders under Rule 58; a Rule 59 motion to alter or amend; denial of a Rule 59 new-trial motion; and a Rule 60 motion filed within 28 days after judgment (or timely under Rule 60(b) if attacking a default judgment). Criminal Rules 29(c), 33, 34, and 35(c) motions are also listed.\nA notice filed before such a motion is decided becomes effective when the motion is decided.\nThe label does not control. A PUC motion to alter or amend that tracks Civil Rule 59 tolls under Rule 4(b)(5). But Environmental Division appeals from municipal panels do not get Rule 4(b) tolling (Jersey Street, 2024 VT 20).","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(a)(4), 4(b)(1)–(12); In re Petition of VT Real Estate Holdings 1 LLC, 2026 VT 13, ¶¶ 4–10"},{"t":"Extending or reopening the appeal time","d":"Extension: the superior court may extend the time on a motion filed no later than 30 days after the original deadline, for excusable neglect or good cause. An extension may not run past the later of 30 days after the original deadline or 14 days after the order granting it. A motion filed after the deadline must be noticed to the other parties.\nReopening: in a civil action, if a party did not get notice of entry within 21 days, the court may reopen the time for 14 days. The motion must be filed within 90 days of entry or 14 days of receiving notice, whichever is earlier, and no party may be prejudiced.\nThe Supreme Court itself may not extend the time for a notice of appeal or a motion for permission to appeal unless law or rule authorizes it.","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(c), 4(d), 26(b)(1)"},{"t":"Separate-document rule: 150-day backstop","d":"In civil cases, a judgment is entered for appeal purposes when it is docketed under V.R.C.P. 79(a). If Rule 58(a) requires a separate document, entry happens at the earlier of the separate document or 150 days after docketing. Failing to use a separate document does not invalidate an appeal.\nFrom January 1, 2027, temporary after-hours orders are docketed as of the date the judge signed them.","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(e)(1)–(2); V.R.C.P. 79(a) (eff. Jan. 1, 2027)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals: 14 days after the first notice","d":"If one party timely appeals, any other party may file a notice of appeal in the superior court within 14 days after the first notice was filed, or within the normal appeal time, whichever ends later. If the first notice comes from an inmate under Rule 4(f), the 14 days run from when the superior court dockets it.\nAn appellee who wants any part of the judgment changed must cross-appeal.","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(a)(6), 4(f)(2); Supreme Court guidance on cross-appeals"},{"t":"Inmate filings","d":"An inmate’s notice of appeal is timely if deposited in the institution’s internal mail system, using the legal-mail system if there is one, by the last day. A notarized statement of the deposit date creates a rebuttable presumption.","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(f)(1), (3)"}]],["Briefing",[{"t":"Brief deadlines: 40 / 30 / 21 days","d":"Appellant’s principal brief (and printed case): 40 days after the record is complete. Appellee’s brief: 30 days after the appellant’s brief. Reply brief: 21 days after the appellee’s brief.\nThese periods replaced the old 30/21/14 schedule on September 6, 2022, when the three-day mail rule ended.\nAn appellee who misses the deadline will not be heard at oral argument without permission. If the appellant misses it, the appellee may move to dismiss.","c":"V.R.A.P. 31(a)(1) (as amended eff. Jan. 1, 2026), 31(a)(2)–(3), 31(b); Judiciary news release on Sept. 6, 2022 amendments"},{"t":"Stipulated extensions: once per brief","d":"The parties may stipulate once to extend the time for each type of brief. The stipulation must be signed by all counsel and state the period, the new date, and the reason. No stipulation may extend the time for a notice of appeal, a request for permission to appeal, or briefs in juvenile proceedings under Title 33, Chapters 51 and 53.\nThe 2021 text capped stipulated extensions at 30 days for the appellant’s brief, 21 for the appellee’s, and 14 for replies. The Judiciary reports that those caps were increased in 2022, but the new numbers were not collected. Confirm the current caps before relying on a stipulation.","c":"V.R.A.P. 26(d) (2021 text; 2022 amendment not collected)"},{"t":"Word limits and certificate of compliance","d":"Principal brief: 9,000 words (appellee’s principal brief in a cross-appeal: 12,000). Reply: 4,500 (appellant’s reply in a cross-appeal: 6,000; appellee’s reply in a cross-appeal: 3,000).\nCounts include headings, footnotes, and quotations. Excluded: statement of issues, tables, signature blocks, the certificate of compliance, any addendum of statutes or rules, and the certificate of service. The certificate must give the word count and name the word-processing software.\nSupreme Court staff may reject an e-filed brief that is over the limit, lacks a word count, or is unsigned.","c":"V.R.A.P. 32(a)(4) (2021 text); 2020 V.R.E.F. 5(e)(2)"},{"t":"Brief contents and format","d":"The appellant’s brief must contain, in order: statement of issues (with page references), table of contents and table of authorities, statement of the case with record references and specific claims of error, argument, and conclusion. The appellee need not include a statement of the case.\nCite the printed case if the document is in it; otherwise cite the appeal volume.\nE-filed briefs: text-searchable PDF, 1-inch margins, page numbers centered at the bottom and matching the PDF counter, 13-point proportionally spaced serif font (footnotes too), 1.2 line spacing, and left alignment. Use italics or bold, not underlining or all caps, for emphasis. No live external links; internal links are allowed from January 1, 2027.","c":"V.R.A.P. 28(a)–(b), (e); 32(a)(1)(A)–(E) (as amended eff. Jan. 1, 2027); Supreme Court guidance"},{"t":"Printed case required again (since 2024)","d":"Most appellants must file a printed case at the same time as the principal brief, even where there is an appeal volume. It is a separately filed compilation with sequential page numbers and an index cross-referencing the appeal volume. At a minimum it contains the record of actions, the decision appealed, and the relevant parts of the record.\nNo printed case is required for appeals from small claims, the Human Services Board, or the Employment Security Board, and the Court may waive it. The appellee may file a supplemental printed case with its brief.\nDo not include unnecessary material; the Court may shift the cost of unnecessary parts.","c":"V.R.A.P. 30(a)(1)(B), 30(b), 30(d)(2)(A), 30(e)–(f) (eff. July 1, 2025); Judiciary news release on printed case"}]],["Motions, argument, and decision",[{"t":"Motions practice","d":"Motions must be in writing and state with particularity the grounds and the relief sought. Supporting documents are filed with the motion. Any party may respond within 14 days after service; the Court’s guidance says the movant may reply to a response to a substantive motion within 7 days.\nThe Court may act on procedural motions at any time without waiting for a response. A single justice may decide any motion except one that dismisses or decides the appeal, subject to full-Court review.\nE-filers must file each motion as a separate document and may not combine a response and a new motion.","c":"V.R.A.P. 27(a)–(d); Supreme Court guidance (7-day reply)"},{"t":"Oral argument and decision timing","d":"The case is ready for the calendar after the appellee’s brief is filed, and parties may request argument. Cases are usually heard or conferenced within about 60 days of being ready.\nThree-justice panels usually decide within two business days of argument or conference. Full-Court cases can take months.","c":"V.R.A.P. 34(a); Supreme Court guidance"},{"t":"Reargument: 14 days","d":"A motion for reargument must be filed within 14 days after entry of judgment. The text of Rule 40 was not collected; this comes from the Court’s published timeline.","c":"V.R.A.P. 40 (per Supreme Court timeline; rule text not collected)"},{"t":"Further review","d":"The Vermont Supreme Court is the last word on Vermont law. Review beyond it is available only in the U.S. Supreme Court, and only on federal questions.","c":"Supreme Court guidance"}]],["Interlocutory and discretionary appeals",[{"t":"Permissive interlocutory appeal (civil): 14 days","d":"On motion, the superior court must permit an appeal from an interlocutory order if it finds (A) a controlling question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion and (B) that an immediate appeal may materially advance the end of the litigation. The motion is due within 14 days after entry of the order. The State’s motion in a criminal case is due within 7 business days.\nIf the trial court denies permission, file a motion for permission in the Supreme Court within 14 days of the denial, attaching the order and the denial. Responses are due 14 days after service.\nThe entry fee is paid within 14 days after the Supreme Court accepts the appeal, and the Court may later dismiss the appeal as improvidently granted. Statute authorizes appeals before final judgment on terms the Supreme Court sets by rule.","c":"V.R.A.P. 5(b)(1), (5)–(8); 12 V.S.A. § 2386"},{"t":"Report by agreement","d":"Before final judgment, if all appearing parties agree, the superior court may report a question of law of sufficient importance or doubt to the Supreme Court. In a civil case, the answer must finally dispose of the action in at least one alternative. The order of report states the question and the parties’ agreement on the entry fee. The fee is due within 14 days after the Court accepts the report.","c":"V.R.A.P. 5(a)"},{"t":"Collateral final orders","d":"The superior court may permit an appeal from an order that conclusively decides a disputed question, resolves an important issue separate from the merits, and would be effectively unreviewable after final judgment. Request permission within 14 days after entry.\nIf the trial court denies permission or a stay, it must give the movant a chance to contact a single justice for a stay. A motion for permission may be filed in the Supreme Court within 14 days of the denial.","c":"V.R.A.P. 5.1(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Appeals needing trial-court permission from a final judgment","d":"Where a final judgment is appealable only with the superior court’s permission, move within 14 days of entry. Rule 4(b) tolling applies. If permission is denied, move in the Supreme Court within 14 days.","c":"V.R.A.P. 6(a)(1), (4)"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP rulings","d":"An order granting or denying a special motion to strike is appealable in the same manner as an interlocutory order under V.R.A.P. 5. Whether this makes the appeal one of right was not determined from the collected texts.","c":"12 V.S.A. § 1041(g)"}]],["E-filing and fees in the Supreme Court",[{"t":"Lawyers must e-file; self-represented parties may","d":"Attorneys must file through the Judiciary’s e-filing system (eFile & Serve). Self-represented parties may e-file, mail, deliver, or email filings to supremecourt@vtcourts.gov. A self-represented party who e-filed in the trial court is presumed to keep e-filing on appeal unless the party gives e-filed notice.\nAn appeal opens a new case, which triggers the $14 per-case e-filing fee unless a waiver applies. Failure to pay the Supreme Court entry fee with the notice of appeal is not a ground for rejecting the filing. A separate certificate of service is required if not all parties are served through the system. Only one copy of a document is filed.","c":"2020 V.R.E.F. 3(a), 3(d)(3), 5(b)(4); Supreme Court Filing Documents page; Judiciary E-Filing page"},{"t":"Filing time and rejections","d":"E-filings may be made at any hour and count for a day if submitted before midnight. Supreme Court staff review filings for compliance with the e-filing rules, the appellate rules, and the public-access rules.\nA rejected filing may be corrected within 7 days and keep its original date. A rejection may be appealed to the Court Administrator within 7 days. Your own technology failure is no excuse.","c":"2020 V.R.E.F. 5(c), 5(e)(1)–(7)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil, and most agency appeals)","d":"30 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nFile with the superior court clerk or agency. Jurisdictional (2026 VT 13, ¶ 4).","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(a)(1); 12 V.S.A. § 2383","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"File with the superior court clerk or agency. Jurisdictional (2026 VT 13, ¶ 4)."}},{"t":"State’s notice of appeal in a criminal case","d":"7 days from entry of the judgment or order (business days).\nCounted in business days. 30 days if the sentence is life imprisonment and the defendant has not waived appeal.","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(a)(2)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"Counted in business days. 30 days if the sentence is life imprisonment and the defendant has not waived appeal."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal / other party’s notice","d":"14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the normal period, whichever ends later).\nFile in the superior court.","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(a)(6)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first notice of appeal)","note":"File in the superior court."}},{"t":"Motion to extend time to appeal","d":"30 days from expiration of the original appeal period.\nExcusable neglect or good cause. Extension capped at the later of 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order.","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(d)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the original appeal period)","note":"Excusable neglect or good cause. Extension capped at the later of 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order."}},{"t":"Motion to reopen time to appeal (no notice of entry)","d":"90 days from entry of judgment (or 14 days after receiving notice, if earlier).\nCivil only. Notice not received within 21 days. Reopened period is 14 days.","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(c)","due":{"n":90,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Civil only. Notice not received within 21 days. Reopened period is 14 days."}},{"t":"Rule 60 motion that tolls the appeal period","d":"28 days from entry of judgment.\nA Rule 60(b) motion attacking a default judgment need only be timely under that rule.","c":"V.R.A.P. 4(b)(7)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"A Rule 60(b) motion attacking a default judgment need only be timely under that rule."}},{"t":"Docketing statement (appellant)","d":"14 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nFiled in the Supreme Court. The appellee may file one within 14 days after the appellant’s.","c":"V.R.A.P. 3(e)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Filed in the Supreme Court. The appellee may file one within 14 days after the appellant’s."}},{"t":"Order transcripts","d":"14 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nAt filing of the notice in bail appeals and juvenile abuse/neglect cases (10(b)(4)).","c":"V.R.A.P. 10(b)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"At filing of the notice in bail appeals and juvenile abuse/neglect cases (10(b)(4))."}},{"t":"Appellant’s principal brief and printed case","d":"40 days from notice that the record is complete.\n40 days since Sept. 6, 2022 (formerly 30).","c":"V.R.A.P. 31(a)(1); 30(e)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (notice that the record is complete)","note":"40 days since Sept. 6, 2022 (formerly 30)."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief (and any supplemental printed case)","d":"30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\nFormerly 21 days. The Court’s timeline says ‘after appellant’s brief is filed.’","c":"V.R.A.P. 31(a)(2); 30(e)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s brief)","note":"Formerly 21 days. The Court’s timeline says ‘after appellant’s brief is filed.’"}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"21 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\nFormerly 14 days.","c":"V.R.A.P. 31(a)(3)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellee’s brief)","note":"Formerly 14 days."}},{"t":"Response to a motion","d":"14 days from service of the motion.\nReply to a response on a substantive motion: 7 days (Court guidance).","c":"V.R.A.P. 27(a)(3)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":"Reply to a response on a substantive motion: 7 days (Court guidance)."}},{"t":"Motion for permission to appeal an interlocutory order (trial court)","d":"14 days from entry of the order or ruling.\nState in criminal cases: 7 business days.","c":"V.R.A.P. 5(b)(5)(A); 5.1(a)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order or ruling)","note":"State in criminal cases: 7 business days."}},{"t":"Motion for permission to appeal in the Supreme Court after denial","d":"14 days from entry of the trial court’s denial.\nResponses due 14 days after service.","c":"V.R.A.P. 5(b)(7)(A); 5.1(b)(2); 6(a)(4)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the trial court’s denial)","note":"Responses due 14 days after service."}},{"t":"Entry fee after an interlocutory appeal is accepted","d":"14 days from entry of the order accepting the appeal.","c":"V.R.A.P. 5(a)(3), 5(b)(6)(B), 5.1(b)(5)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order accepting the appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion for reargument","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\nFrom the Court’s timeline; rule text not collected.","c":"V.R.A.P. 40","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"From the Court’s timeline; rule text not collected."}},{"t":"Correct a rejected e-filing (keep original filing date)","d":"7 days from notice of rejection or failed submission.\nAppeal of a rejection to the Court Administrator: 7 days; tolls the correction period.","c":"2020 V.R.E.F. 5(d)(4)–(5), 5(e)(4)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (notice of rejection or failed submission)","note":"Appeal of a rejection to the Court Administrator: 7 days; tolls the correction period."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/"]]},"rulesName":"Vermont Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"4(c)","t":"Process: by whom served","s":"Process is served by a sheriff, deputy sheriff, constable, or other person authorized by law, or by an indifferent person specially appointed by a superior judge. Mail service under (d)(2) or (f), and waiver requests under (l), may be mailed by the plaintiff or counsel; subpoenas are served under Rule 45. Since January 1, 2026, special appointments must be made freely and promptly on motion without notice if service will be more economical or efficient than by an officer, replacing the old ‘substantial savings in travel fees’ test. The appointee must serve the appointment order with the papers.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"4(e)","t":"Service outside the state","s":"Cross-referenced in the Rule 55(a) order: service outside Vermont may be made in the manner required in Vermont or in any manner allowed by the law of the state where the person is served. Full text not collected.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other documents","s":"Service between e-filers with service contacts must go through the e-filing system, unless the parties agree otherwise under V.R.E.F. 11(d)(2). Non-e-filers may be served by delivery or mail, or by email if they consented in their notice of appearance, agreed in a filed writing, or have no known physical or postal address but listed an email in a filing. E-filers must serve discovery through the system unless otherwise agreed. Discovery is not filed unless the court orders it or it is used in the proceeding. Email filing is for those not required to e-file and is timely if sent before midnight. Mail and delivery filings count when received before the clerk’s office closes. Inmate filings count when deposited in the institution’s mail. Every document served after the complaint needs a certificate of service, but a missing certificate is not a ground to refuse the filing. (Text as amended effective Sept. 6, 2022.)","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"6(a)(4)","t":"Time: ‘last day’ defined","s":"Unless a statute or court order sets a different time, the last day ends at midnight in the court’s time zone for email and e-filing, and when the clerk’s office is scheduled to close for other filing methods. (Eff. Sept. 6, 2022.)","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"6(e)","t":"Additional time after mail service (abolished)","s":"The rule adding 3 days when a period runs from service by mail was eliminated across all divisions and the Supreme Court effective September 6, 2022, according to the Judiciary’s announcement. Unlike FRCP 6(d), Vermont adds no extra days. The current text of Rule 6(e) was not collected.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"9.2","t":"CARES Act notice requirement (eviction)","s":"Adopted effective October 1, 2022 and amended effective July 1, 2024, to enforce the notice requirement of 15 U.S.C. § 9058(c)(1) (CARES Act). Made permanent effective October 1, 2025. The rule text itself was not collected.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"11(e)","t":"Use of declaration in place of notarization (deleted)","s":"Deleted effective July 1, 2025. It is replaced by Rule 43(h), which allows declarations without exception wherever a rule or order requires an affidavit or sworn statement.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"16.2","t":"Scheduling orders","s":"The court may enter a scheduling order on motion or stipulation, or after a status conference, discovery conference, or hearing. A conference is no longer required. The order may set deadlines for pretrial motions (except those based on later events or lack of subject-matter jurisdiction), third-party practice under Rule 14, e-discovery, privilege clawback agreements, and the close of all discovery, plus ‘other appropriate matters’ such as a trial date. A continuance or extension requires a motion and good cause, but the court must modify the order to prevent manifest injustice. A scheduling order overrides other rule-based timing. Violations can bring Rule 37(b)(2)(B)–(C) sanctions, or dismissal or default for failing to appear for trial. (Eff. July 1, 2025.) Unlike FRCP 16(b), the collected text contains no mandatory scheduling-order deadline.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"16.3","t":"Mediation","s":"Cross-referenced in Rule 26(f): if Rule 16.3 requires mediation, the discovery-conference order must set a date for completing it. Full text not collected.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"26(f)","t":"Discovery conference","s":"The court may call a discovery conference at any time, and must do so on a motion that includes the issues, a proposed discovery plan (including ESI), proposed limits, other proposed orders, and a statement of good-faith efforts to agree. Objections or additions are due 14 days after service of the motion. The resulting order is a Rule 16.2 scheduling order and must set a date to complete any Rule 16.3 mediation. It can be changed only under Rule 16.2(c). (Eff. July 1, 2025.) Unlike FRCP 26(f), a conference is held on motion or court order rather than required in every case.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","s":"Unless a judge orders otherwise, parties may by written stipulation set deposition logistics and modify discovery procedures. All discovery must be served as provided in Rule 5(b)(2)(D). (Eff. Sept. 6, 2022.)","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"43(d)","t":"Affirmation in place of oath","s":"Whenever a rule or court order requires a person to take an oath, the court may accept a solemn affirmation instead. (Reworded eff. July 1, 2025.)","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"43(h)","t":"Declaration in place of affidavit or oath","s":"Wherever a rule or court order requires an affidavit or a statement under oath, a party may file or serve a document signed under penalty of perjury with the prescribed declaration language above the signature. It has no exceptions, but it does not cover acknowledgments or other notarizations. Effective July 1, 2025; replaces former Rule 43(h) (order of evidence, abrogated).","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"55(a)","t":"Default: motion for default judgment","s":"When a party has failed to plead or otherwise defend, the party seeking affirmative relief may move for default judgment. From January 1, 2027, a motion that relies on service outside Vermont made under the law of the place of service must cite the statute or rule of that place that authorizes the method used.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"The movant must file a separate, concise statement of undisputed material facts in numbered paragraphs with specific record citations. The opponent must respond paragraph by paragraph, reproducing each numbered paragraph, and may file a separate statement of additional facts. Each side must give the other an editable copy on request. Affidavits or declarations must be on personal knowledge, set out admissible facts, and show competence. Under (d), a nonmovant who shows by affidavit or declaration that it cannot yet present essential facts may get deferral, denial, or time for discovery. Under (h), bad-faith affidavits or declarations can bring fee-shifting and contempt. (Subdivisions (c)(1), (2), (6), (d), and (h) as amended eff. July 1, 2025.) The 2025 Reporter’s Notes say Rule 56 has been based on the 2010 FRCP 56 since 2012. The statement-of-facts procedure in (c)(1)–(2) has no counterpart in the federal rule text.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"65(b)(1)","t":"Preliminary injunction: notice","s":"The court may issue a preliminary injunction only on motion, after notice and a hearing at a time and place set by the clerk. The notice may be signed by the clerk or the court. The movant must serve the notice of hearing together with the motion on all parties. Effective January 1, 2026, the former option to request a preliminary injunction in the complaint without a motion is eliminated.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"79(a)","t":"Civil docket","s":"The clerk keeps the civil docket, grouping civil and small claims cases with consecutive file numbers and entering papers, process, appearances, orders, verdicts, and judgments chronologically. Each entry of an order or judgment shows the date of entry. From January 1, 2027, a temporary after-hours order is entered as of the date the judge signed it. This matters for appeal timing under V.R.A.P. 4(e).","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"79.1","t":"Appearance and withdrawal of attorneys and self-represented parties","s":"A signature on a pleading is an appearance. Appearances made in open court must be confirmed in writing and served within 7 days, and must give a postal and email address. Self-represented parties file a notice-of-appearance form, give an email if they have one, and say whether they consent to email service. (e) Pro hac vice: a lawyer in good standing elsewhere who has filed an A.O. 41, § 16 licensing statement and paid the fee may be admitted for a particular case, in the court’s discretion. This is on motion of an associated Vermont lawyer, who must sign all filings and receive service. (f)–(g) Withdrawal needs leave of court. After withdrawal, the party has 30 days from notice to appear or face dismissal or default. (h) Limited appearances are allowed for listed purposes. (Text as amended eff. Sept. 6, 2022; former (i) eCabinet-number requirement deleted.)","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"80.10(c)","t":"Orders against sexual assault: after-hours relief","s":"Cross-referenced in the Rule 79(a) Reporter’s Notes as the source of ex parte temporary orders available outside regular court hours (see 12 V.S.A. § 5134(a)). Full text not collected.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"80.12","t":"Procedures for post-conviction relief","s":"New effective January 1, 2027. Governs Civil Division petitions under 13 V.S.A. §§ 7131–7137 by persons in custody under a Vermont sentence. The petition may be informal but must give the case number, sentencing date, and the alleged defect. File in the county where the sentence was imposed, with a separate petition for each county, and list any prior challenge. The reviewing judge may not be the sentencing judge. The petition is dismissed on preliminary review if it plainly shows no entitlement to relief; otherwise the clerk serves the State’s Attorney, Attorney General, and Defender General. No answer is required unless ordered. Discovery is allowed for good cause. An evidentiary hearing is held under the Rules of Evidence. Stipulated relief may be accepted or rejected but not modified. Relief on the merits vacates the judgment and remands to the Criminal Division. Final orders are appealable.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"},{"n":"81(a)","t":"Applicability: limited applicability","s":"The civil rules do not alter statutory practice for commencing and conducting habeas corpus proceedings. Where the statute is silent, the rules apply, but discovery requires a court order on motion for good cause. From January 1, 2027, the former reference to post-conviction relief proceedings is removed because Rule 80.12 now governs them.","u":"https://www.vermontjudiciary.org/attorneys/rules/promulgated"}]});
